{"records":[{"id":"x8kbx5hmxs","title":"Eukaryotic clathrin adapter protein and mediator of cholesterol homeostasis, PICALM, affects trafficking to the chlamydial inclusion","description":"The obligate intracellular pathogen Chlamydia trachomatis has unique metabolic requirements as it proceeds through its biphasic developmental cycle from within the inclusion within the host cell. In our previous study, we identified a host protein, PICALM, which localizes to the chlamydial inclusion. PICALM functions in many host pathways including the recycling of receptors, specific SNARE proteins, and molecules like transferrin, and maintaining cholesterol homeostasis. Hence, we hypothesized that PICALM functions to maintain the cholesterol content and to moderate trafficking from the endosomal recycling pathway to the inclusion, which controls chlamydial access to this pathway. In uninfected cells, siRNA knockdown of PICALM resulted in increased cholesterol within the Golgi and transferrin receptor (TfR) positive vesicles (recycling endosomes). PICALM knockdown in cells infected with C. trachomatis resulted in increased levels of Golgi-derived lipid and protein, TfR, transferrin, and Rab11-FIP1 localized to inclusions and a decrease of Golgi fragmentation at and Rab11 trafficking to the inclusion. Interestingly, chlamydial infection alone also increases cholesterol in TfR and Rab11-associated vesicles, and PICALM knockdown reverses this effect. Our data suggest that PICALM functions to balance or limit chlamydial access to multiple subcellular trafficking pathways to maintain the health of the host cell during chlamydial infection.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-20T10:22:16.789Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/x8kbx5hmxs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x8kbx5hmxs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Elizabeth","last_name":"Rucks","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"R01AI114670"}],"categories":["Microbiology","Cell Biology","Intracellular Trafficking","Bacterial Pathogen"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sctxy3sm9w","title":"Generation and characterization of human iPSC-derived NPC1I1061T/I10161T i3Neurons as a model for NPC1 disease","description":"This manuscript describes the generation of an inducible pluripotent stem cell (iPSC) line expressing a mutant form of NPC1. This variant, NPC1(I1061T/I1061T), represents a common pathogenic variant of NPC1 in NPC1 affected individuals. Upon differentiation of the iPSCs into i3Neurons, we characterized the cellular phenotype with respect to gycosylation of NPC1, accumulation of free cholesterol, and response to known therapeutics. The results demonstrate that this cell line exhibits the expected characteristics of the NPC1(I1061/I1061T) mutant protein resulting in an NPC1 disease phenotype in these cells. This new cell line can be used as a neuronal model of Niemann-Pick disease, type C1.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-02-04T20:45:50.416Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sctxy3sm9w","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sctxy3sm9w"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Niamh","last_name":"Cawley","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Forbes","last_name":"Porter","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shikha","last_name":"Salhotra","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"NICHD Intramural Research Program of the National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"ZIA HD008988"}],"categories":["Niemann-Pick Disease"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fw3g6w68xk","title":"miR-25","description":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb) is the main pathogen causing tuberculosis (TB). Although autophagy is an important regulatory pathway to eliminate intracellular Mtb, the effect of NPC1 during Mtb infection remains largely unclear. Here, we found that microRNA-25 (miR-25) expression was significantly upregulated during in the lung tissues of mice infected with Bacillus Calmette-Guerin (BCG) and macrophages infected with Mtb or BCG, especially in the early stages of infection. We validated that miR-25 targets the NPC1 protein located on the lysosomal membrane, resulting in damage to lysosomal function, thereby inhibiting autophagolysosome formation and promoting the survival of Mtb and BCG. Consistently, mice lacking miR-25 exhibited more resistant to BCG infection. In addition, we found that Rv1759c induces the expression of miR-25 through NFKB inhibitor zeta (NFKBIZ). Together, we reveal a new mechanism by which Mtb regulates NPC1 to influence lysosomal function and increase the survival of Mtb through the autophagy pathway.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-22T14:30:09.255Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fw3g6w68xk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fw3g6w68xk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"wenqi","last_name":"dong","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["China"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6mcj5n2xwg","title":"Diehl ISCIENCE-D-21-01407R1","description":"These are data files for our manuscript that was recently submitted to iScience. \nThe title of our manuscript is \"Hepatocyte Activity of the Cholesterol Sensor Smoothened Regulates Cholesterol and Bile Acid Homeostasis in Mice\". ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-09-01T07:42:32.557Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6mcj5n2xwg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6mcj5n2xwg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"George","last_name":"Dalton","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Applied Sciences","Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"v7gdh9prbj","title":"mTOR inhibition reprograms cellular lipid homeostasis by inducing alternative lipid uptake and promoting cholesterol transport. Shin et al.","description":"Raw data files for the manuscript entitled 'mTOR inhibition reprograms cellular lipid homeostasis by inducing alternative lipid uptake and promoting cholesterol transport.'\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-01T15:36:01.953Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v7gdh9prbj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v7gdh9prbj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sang-Oh","last_name":"Yoon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sejeong","last_name":"Shin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Signaling","Cholesterol"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"knmfx9t732","title":"CELL-D-16-01418R3_Widenmaier et al.","description":"Cholesterol is a critical nutrient requiring tight constraint in the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) due to its uniquely challenging properties. While the mechanisms by which ER defends against cholesterol insufficiency are well described, it remains unclear how ER senses and effectively defends against cholesterol excess. Here, we identify the ER-bound transcription factor nuclear factor erythroid 2 related factor-1, Nrf1/Nfe2L1, as a critical mediator of this process. We show Nrf1 directly binds to and specifically senses cholesterol in the ER through a defined domain, and that cholesterol regulates Nrf1 turnover, processing, localization, and activity. In Nrf1 deficiency, in vivo cholesterol-challenges induce massive hepatic cholesterol accumulation and damage, which is rescued by replacing Nrf1 exogenously. This Nrf1-mediated mechanism involves the suppression of CD36-driven inflammatory signaling and de-repression of liver X receptor activity. These findings reveal Nrf1 as a guardian of cholesterol homeostasis and a core component of adaptive responses to excess cellular cholesterol.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-10-11T04:15:10.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/knmfx9t732","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/knmfx9t732"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Widenmaier","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biological Sciences","Cholesterol","Fatty Liver"],"journal_issn":["00928674"]},{"id":"63wx3vd7hx","title":"iScience","description":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Mtb) evades host clearance by inhibiting autophagy. MicroRNA-25 (miR-25) expression was significantly up-regulated in the lung tissues of mice infected with Bacillus Calmette-Guerin (BCG) and macrophages infected with Mtb or BCG, especially in the early stages of infection. MiR-25 can significantly increase the survival of Mtb and BCG in macrophages. We validated that miR-25 targets the NPC1 protein located on the lysosomal membrane, resulting in damage to lysosomal function, thereby inhibiting autophagolysosome formation and promoting the survival of Mtb and BCG. Consistently, mice lacking miR-25 exhibited more resistant to BCG infection. In addition, we found that Rv1759c induces the expression of miR-25 through NFKB inhibitor zeta (NFKBIZ). This study demonstrates that the role of miR-25 during Mtb infection contributes to a better understanding of the pathogenesis of tuberculosis (TB).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-16T18:15:59.891Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/63wx3vd7hx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/63wx3vd7hx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"wenqi","last_name":"dong","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"44ky4hzhcw","title":"LXR stimulates a metabolic switch and reveals cholesterol homeostasis as a statin-target against Tasmanian Devil Facial Tumor Disease","description":"This is raw data related to the manuscript \"LXR stimulates a metabolic switch and reveals cholesterol homeostasis as a statin-target against Tasmanian Devil Facial Tumor Disease\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-16T11:06:09.046Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/44ky4hzhcw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/44ky4hzhcw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Manuel","last_name":"Fernandez-Rojo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Maria","last_name":"Ikonomopoulou","orcid":"0000-0001-7739-080X"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yjkdx4byf3","title":"ESCRT-I complex enhances therapeutic efficacy of antisense oligonucleotide drugs by regulating endosomal escape and glucose homeostasis","description":"Antisense oligonucleotide (ASO) therapeutics silence gene expression through RNase H–mediated mRNA degradation or steric blockade, yet their clinical efficacy is limited by inefficient intracellular trafficking, with <1% of internalized ASOs escaping endosomes to access target RNA. A deeper understanding of the cellular mechanisms governing ASO trafficking and activity is therefore critical. Here, we identify the Endosomal Sorting Complex Required for Transport-I (ESCRT-I) as a previously unrecognized regulator of ASO pharmacological activity. Using human hepatocyte-derived cell models and FDA-approved ASO drugs inotersen and mipomersen, we demonstrate that suppression of specific ESCRT-I subunits, VPS23 and VPS28, markedly enhances ASO-mediated target silencing, whereas depletion of other subunits has minimal effect. Mechanistically, VPS23 functions as a central ESCRT-I component linking endosomal trafficking to intracellular glucose homeostasis. Loss of VPS23 reduces expression of the glucose transporter GLUT2, lowers intracellular glucose levels, and promotes ASO endosomal escape without altering cellular uptake or RNase H1–dependent activity. VPS23 suppression disrupts endosomal morphology, decreases ASO retention in late endosomes, and enhances cytosolic availability of ASOs. Notably, this regulatory effect extends beyond ASOs to siRNA therapeutics, indicating a broader role for ESCRT-I in RNA drug biology. Together, these findings uncover a metabolic–endosomal axis controlling nucleic acid drug efficacy and provide new mechanistic insight into intracellular determinants of RNA-based therapeutics.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-24T12:46:11.683Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yjkdx4byf3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yjkdx4byf3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Le Tra Giang","last_name":"Nguyen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Protein","RNA","Real-Time Polymerase Chain Reaction","Immunofluorescence"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r93jptbhr2","title":"LXR stimulates a metabolic switch and reveals cholesterol homeostasis as a statin-target against Tasmanian Devil Facial Tumor Disease","description":"This is raw experimental data (western-blots) available for the publication \"LXR stimulates a metabolic switch and reveals cholesterol homeostasis as a statin-target against Tasmanian Devil Facial Tumor Disease.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-08T10:36:01.448Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r93jptbhr2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r93jptbhr2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Manuel Alejandro","last_name":"Fernandez-Rojo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":125527,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":140,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":125527,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":124953,"restricted":64,"embargoed":510},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2185},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":654},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":443},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":405},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":399},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":387},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":385},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":326},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":258},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":215}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":123005},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1811},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":179},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":126},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":76},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":53},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":39},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":26}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":473},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":405},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":357},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":318},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":285}]}}